  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "age-05-product-and-quadratic-age-problems",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Age Products and Quadratic Relations",
    "description": "Handle age products without losing the timeline: reduce to one variable, factor, keep valid ages, then answer the dated question.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with a concrete age situation before the equation, label every past/present/future date clearly, show the reason for each age shift, and check the result in the original clue. After each worked example, give a fresh near-transfer question. Wrong answers receive a misconception-specific correction and a parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 31,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Teach age problems whose equations are nonlinear because ages are multiplied. Start with a ratio plus an age product, then product plus a linear relation, friendly sum-and-product factor pairs, dated products that require shifting both ages before multiplying, and a fixed age difference plus a product. Explain why negative algebraic roots are rejected in age contexts. After actual ages are found, apply past/future shifts or ratios. Keep quadratic work at the factorable level used in the chapter and always distinguish the scale factor from the requested age."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Some age questions use products. They look harder because two unknown ages are multiplied, but a ratio can turn both ages into one variable.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose a man and son are in ratio 5:2 and their age product is 360.\n\nLet ages be 5x and 2x.\nProduct: 10x² = 360.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "10x²=360 gives x²=36, so x=6. Their ages are 30 and 12.\n\nCheck: 30:12=5:2 and 30×12=360.\n\nThe pattern is ratio → one variable → product → positive root → actual ages.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
